  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "SIM-zazit is mainly about CID/chunk replication, not human-readable reference naming. It gives useful boundary evidence that L5 feeds should carry roots, chunks, and pointer objects rather than become a naming registry, but it does not define scope rules for collisions like Alice.release vs Bob.release.",
    "strengths": [
      "Strong no-central-authority and sparse-knowledge posture via CID-first replication and peer-local pull/accounting inputs.",
      "Very clear layer boundary between meaning-oblivious feed behavior and higher-layer semantics.",
      "Docs are explicit enough to audit why naming pressure is mostly out of scope here."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No explicit namespace, scope, or authority model for human-readable labels such as `release`.",
      "No local evidence flow for competing name claims from Alice and Bob.",
      "No naming-focused evolution or migration story."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A later layer may quietly reintroduce an implicit global namespace if scoped naming rules are not added.",
      "Operators could mistake replicated roots or pointer objects for a complete answer to human reference naming."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which layer should bind human-readable names to roots or pointer objects?",
      "Should name scope be peer-local, group-scoped, site-scoped, or attached to a signed authority object?",
      "What peer-local audit record should show why Bob accepted one `release` claim over another?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
